 - **Copy-mode projects** (Option B) already have their hooks in `.claude/settings.json`, so their gates run in the cloud as well.
 - **This repository** registers every hook from `hooks/hooks.json` in its own `.claude/settings.json`, generated by `scripts/sync_cloud_hooks.py` (#520). Each entry only starts when `CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE` is exactly `true`; locally the command exits 0 immediately, so the plugin's hooks never run twice. In the cloud, `session_singleton_guard.py` skips the worktree requirement — the container is the isolation.
 - After every change to `hooks/hooks.json`, run `python3 scripts/sync_cloud_hooks.py` and commit the result. `python3 scripts/sync_cloud_hooks.py --check` reports drift, and `scripts/release_check.py` (line "Cloud-Hooks") blocks a release on drift.
-- **Short aliases** in this repository deliver the command text in the cloud through the inline-bash fallback of the redirect aliases (local `skills/<name>/SKILL.md`); plugin-mode consumers only get its stop text `BEFEHLSTEXT FEHLT` (#522).
-- **Plugin-mode consumers** have no gates in cloud sessions yet: the hook scripts are not part of their repository. Follow-up: #522.
+- **Short aliases** in this repository deliver the command text in the cloud through the inline-bash fallback of the redirect aliases (local `skills/<name>/SKILL.md`); plugin-mode consumers only get its stop text `BEFEHLSTEXT FEHLT` (shims and aliases for consumers: #562).
+
+### Plugin-mode consumers (#522)
+
+The hook scripts are not part of a plugin consumer's repository, so the cloud needs a second way to load them:
+
+1. Run once: `python3 /path/to/agent-os-openspec/setup.py <project> --cloud-gates` (plugin-mode projects only; copy-mode projects are rejected with a hint because they already have their hooks).
+2. Commit the three changed files: `.claude/cloud_bootstrap.py` (small start script, standard library only), `.claude/settings.json` (every hook from `hooks/hooks.json`, each guarded by `CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE=true` and started with `python3 -I`, so a file in `.claude/` or on `PYTHONPATH` cannot replace a standard module; timeout + 25 s; your own hooks, `enabledPlugins`, `permissions` and `env` stay untouched) and `.claude/framework_version.json` (`cloud_ref` = release tag `agent-os-openspec--vX.Y.Z`, `cloud_sha` = its commit).
+3. In a cloud session, the first hook call clones exactly that tag into `~/.cache/agent-os-openspec/<tag>`, checks the commit and runs the hooks from there; before every call it checks that the cache is untouched (no symlinks, `HEAD` equals the pin, clean working tree) and re-fetches it otherwise. Locally the entries exit 0 immediately, so nothing runs twice next to the plugin.
+4. Raise the pin with `setup.py <project> --update`; it keeps and refreshes `cloud_ref`/`cloud_sha`, the start script and the entries (projects without a pin stay unchanged).
+
+Requirement: the cloud environment must reach `github.com`. If the fetch fails (no network, tag missing, commit mismatch), the session runs fail-closed: only reading tools (`Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, read-only `git status|diff|log|show`, `ls`, `cat`, …) work, writing and outbound tools are blocked, and SessionStart shows a warning. Fix the network and run `python3 .claude/cloud_bootstrap.py --retry`. As a last resort the PO types `override` as the whole message: writing tools are released for this session for one hour, logged in `.claude/gate-events.jsonl`.
+
+Limits: while the gates are not loaded, the secret guards do not check reading tools; cloud "project threads" do not run repository hooks; setting `CLAUDE_CODE_REMOTE=true` locally by hand causes double runs; the pin lags behind the plugin's `autoUpdate` until `--update` runs. The Bash protection of `.claude/cloud_bootstrap.py` and `.claude/framework_version.json` is a heuristic like the other path rules of `bash_gate.py`: globs, brace expansion or `python -c` writes are not recognised. If the harness delivers unreadable hook input for `PreToolUse` while the gates are not loaded, the start script lets the call pass (exit 0). The cache check hashes every file itself against `git ls-tree` of the pinned commit, so it does not detect deliberately forged object files in the cache repository (that requires tampering with `.git/objects` on purpose). Checking the cache and starting the hook are not atomic: a background program deliberately started by the model that rewrites the cache under `~/.cache/agent-os-openspec/` during a hook call can exploit that gap (follow-up issue #564).
 
